The code above might fix the problem from the "old" 0.75 found on rutgers,
but there is *definitely* no save-game problem with the ftp.uu.net sources.
I don't know why Kevin Thomas asserts there still is above, unless he's
trying to imply I can't tell when I'm running on a UNIX system.

Those sources can be found on ftp.uu.net in
/usenet/comp.sources.games/volume7/omega3.

There are 2 sections
L294==================================================================
  for (i=0;i<TOTALITEMS;i++)
    fwrite((char *)&(Objects[i].known),sizeof(Objects[i].known),1,fd);
L609==================================================================
  for (i=0;i<TOTALITEMS;i++) 
    fread((char *)&(Objects[i].known),sizeof(Objects[i].known),1,fd);
======================================================================
This is the correct code, as it appears in the archive.
It works properly on all UNIX systems I've ever heard of (should even work on
Kevin's), as well as MSDOS systems.

For those of you who are C programmers, it should be clear that adding
in the #ifndef described earlier to the first of the two code fragments
above cannot have a beneficial effect. (It might have a harmful one, though.)

>*** .orig/osave.c       Thu May  4 16:06:41 1989
Also, note the date. The date on all the shar archive files at ftp.uu.net is
7/20/89. Under normal use, there's no way Kevin could have gotten the version
from ftp.uu.net recently and have a date from before the files were put on the
archive. I think Kevin made a mistake.

Let's let it rest now, okay?
